"""Archived public literature cards for the legacy builder; no private chat context."""
def populate(md):
    md("### E1. Lexical representation can matter within the cosine family\n\n[Hoberg–Phillips (2016), Table 5](https://doi.org/10.1086/688176) reports named-rival recovery of 47.1% for SIC3, 40.9% for all words without common-word removal, 52.5% for nouns/proper nouns with a 25% frequency exclusion, 62.0% with a 10% exclusion, and 59.3% for all-word TF-IDF. This compares 2008 classifications with 2011 CapIQ rival labels. It demonstrates sensitivity to representation and filtering, not precision@5 or optimal cutoffs for 560 companies. The original filing-based construction cannot silently be imported as target-side evidence under our restrictions.\n\n### E2. The current encoder is designed for cosine, but not our company objective\n\nThe official [`all-mpnet-base-v2` model card](https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2) describes contrastive sentence-pair training using cosine similarity and truncation beyond 384 wordpieces by default. Thus cosine is a technically coherent pairing with this encoder. It is not a validation of whole-company comparability. A 200-word chunk is not guaranteed to stay below 384 wordpieces, so effective tokenization/truncation should be checked on real inputs.\n\n### E3. A caution about treating vector angles as intrinsic meaning\n\n[Steck and colleagues (2024)](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.05440v1) show that cosine behavior in linear matrix-factorization settings can depend on regularization choices and may be arbitrary in specified settings. This is a warning to validate the learned representation and geometry against the intended task. It is not proof that contrastively trained MPNet embeddings are invalid or that another metric is automatically superior.\n\n### E4. Company embeddings and financial proxies\n\n[BlackRock company-similarity research (2023)](https://arxiv.org/html/2308.08031v1) reports a k=5 stock-return-correlation proxy of .443 for pretrained SBERT and .438 for its GICS-fine-tuned version. This is not human whole-company relevance, and the small difference is not treated here as significant. Its use of 2022 descriptions with earlier return observations further limits historical interpretation. More training complexity did not automatically improve that measured proxy.\n\n### E5. Language models versus named peers\n\n[Cao et al. (2025), Appendix 2](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11142-025-09892-6) studies the 40 largest SIC 7372 firms with three experts having over 20 years' experience. Experts average 159 total peer assignments across those 40 firms, approximately four per target. LLM/expert mean overlap is 42.1%, versus 10.7% for TNIC; expert mutual overlaps range from 47.8% to 64.8%. These are overlap measures, not accuracy or an MPNet comparison, and retrospective 2021 judgments were collected in 2024. The paper's Etsy illustration contrasts LLM peers eBay/Shopify/Amazon with TNIC peers Revolve/Twilio/Yelp; these are its examples, not our approved peer labels.\n\n### E6. Open LLM-peer data is a reference, not human gold\n\nThe authors' [LLM_Peers repository](https://github.com/ycao25/LLM_Peers) provides a July 2023 Bard dataset with 2,639,157 peer pairs and 344,837 firm-years, plus a separate Gemini 2 Flash resource covering 1981–2023. GVKEY mapping supports entity linkage. Model-generated historical labels are not proof of point-in-time knowledge or independent human relevance. Use as an external candidate/reference source with declared provenance, never as unquestioned truth.\n\n### E7. Pairwise LLM ranking is an algorithmic precedent\n\n[Qin et al. (2024)](https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-naacl.97/) evaluates pairwise language-model ranking for document retrieval. It supports considering pairwise comparison rather than relying exclusively on one embedding vector. Applying it to company dossiers, reversing candidate order, preserving an unknown option and requiring source citations are proposed adaptations. This paper does not establish company-comparability performance.\n\n### E8. Company graphs add information, not merely a different distance\n\n[EQT CompanyKG v3](https://arxiv.org/html/2306.10649v3) reports 76 competitor-retrieval deep dives: Recall@50 is 43.69% for weighted shortest path, 12.96% for mSBERT and 14.09% for ADA2. Yet its separate similarity-ranking accuracy is 61.16% for weighted shortest path versus 67.14% for mSBERT; GraphMAE with mSBERT reaches 67.61%. The winner depends on the task. Graph inputs include proprietary relationships, including competitors, so this is not an equal-information metric comparison. Anonymized nodes cannot simply be joined to our company names.\n\n### E9. Supervised valuation can improve valuation, under another information set\n\n[Geertsema–Lu, Journal of Accounting Research](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/1475-679X.12464), Table 4, uses the same 1,335,453 observations and ten features: median absolute equity error is 52% for OLS, 40% for log-target OLS and 33% for log-target GBM. This separates transformation and learner effects. Table 2's GBM .29–.32 versus TNIC .40–.59 involves different samples and up to 60 peers; it is not our head-to-head. Moderna's illustrated peers include LiveRamp/TiVo and biotech firms, exposing valuation/business-objective differences. Negative learned weights and direct predictions are not a top-five peer median.\n\nThe [replication repository](https://github.com/helenhelu/RelativeValuationWithMachineLearning) relies on proprietary CRSP/Compustat inputs. The related [HBR five-tree demonstration](https://github.com/pgeertsema/HBR_peers) is in-sample teaching material, not independent validation or a ready project dataset.\n\n### E10. Valuation-supervised peer selection: relevant research, unresolved transfer\n\n[Frank et al. (August 2026 preprint)](https://arxiv.org/html/2608.12594v1), all BlackRock-affiliated, learn weighted CatBoost leaf co-occurrence from post-money equity valuations. Their 270,000-company universe supplies roughly 53,000 latest-transaction labels and 27 features, predominantly categorical. This is not an EV/EBITDA model. The paper specifies an 80/20 subindustry-stratified split and training-only preprocessing, fitting, weighting and calibration; it does not establish temporal validation. Table 4 reports OLS/CatBoost MAE 1.19/1.08 and RMSE 1.56/1.44, not gains over cosine. Financial Services Figure 4 favors learned proximity; exact plot values were not verified. Structured features versus internal-description embeddings change information as well as method. Latest financials paired with historical deals, percentage-error units and the conformal evaluation denominator need clarification. No public dataset/model release, deployed-product evaluation or independent whole-business peer approval is demonstrated.\n\nReproduction also needs the exact growth policy: the stated symmetric-tree description and leaf constraints require reconciliation with [CatBoost's parameter documentation](https://catboost.ai/docs/en/references/training-parameters/common#grow_policy). This background review neither changes the frozen experiment nor establishes a local winner.\n\n### E11. Candidate universe changes what can be retrieved\n\n[Global Business Networks, JFE (2025)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2025.104007) illustrates Ford in 2021: US-universe OpenAI-L peers include GM, BorgWarner, Ally, GE and Microsoft; global peers include GM, Honda, Hyundai, Nissan and Toyota. The example restricts candidates to NYSE size decile eight or higher. It demonstrates the importance of candidate availability, not unbiased retrieval accuracy or a recommended financial filter for this project.\n\n### E12. Business scope in enterprise data\n\n[FactSet's August 2018 RBICS overview](https://insight.factset.com/resources/at-a-glance-factset-rbics-with-tradenames-datafeed) illustrates wearables as 100% of Fitbit and 24.69% of Garmin. This historical example makes business breadth concrete. Those proportions are neither current facts nor justified weights for our dataset. Revenue-exposure taxonomies motivate preserving company scope; their commercial existence does not establish our scoring accuracy.\n\n### E13. Operating-model filters in a customer workflow\n\nThe [Grata/Brenntag case](https://grata.com/case-studies/how-grata-helps-brenntag-screen-m-a-targets-efficiently) reports narrowing roughly 15,000 targets to 500 within minutes using a few filters, including business-model distinctions. It is a named customer efficiency claim published by the vendor, not independent precision or a controlled cosine comparison. It shows a practical use for operating-role information.\n\n### E14. Transport supplies an alignment mechanism\n\n[Word Mover's Distance](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v37/kusnerb15.pdf) uses balanced transport: both marginal distributions are conserved and mass may split, so it is not one-to-one matching. Its classification results favor WMD on six of eight datasets, not company-peer tasks. Relaxed WMD drops one marginal constraint; nearest matching can ignore destination proportions and is a lower bound, not full portfolio alignment. [Sentence Mover's Similarity](https://aclanthology.org/P19-1264/) extends transport to sentence representations for text evaluation.\n\n[Cuturi (2013)](https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2013/file/af21d0c97db2e27e13572cbf59eb343d-Paper.pdf) uses entropy regularization and Sinkhorn computation; speed comes with a changed objective, and the reported MNIST evidence is not company validation. [Chizat et al.](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.05816) allow marginal deviations penalized through divergences such as KL or total variation. Unmatched mass is not free; this differs from partial transport. Activity accuracy, meaningful masses and ground costs determine transfer. Website frequency is not revenue share. None of these studies establishes an OT win for our company task.\n\n### E15. Method selection can itself overfit\n\n[Cawley–Talbot (2010)](https://jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html) analyzes overfitting in model selection and resulting evaluation bias. The implication is to preserve genuinely unused evaluation data and account for the selection process. It is not a reason to avoid multiple substantive methods; it is a reason not to use the same labels for choosing and declaring the winner.\n\n### E16. Financial rank distance can compete with industry peers\n\n[Plenborg and colleagues (2018), Tables 3–4](https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/portalfiles/portal/57344051/thomas_plenborg_et_al_multiv_rdians_ttelse_publishersversion.pdf) compare six-peer methods over 12,350 S&P 1500 firm-years. Median absolute percentage valuation error using EV/EBIT is 25.5% for industry, 22.2% for SARD and 20.3% for SARD within industry; EV/sales values are 40.7%, 25.4% and 27.1%. Factors are ROE, net debt/EBIT, market capitalization, forecast growth and EBIT margin. Private-target market capitalization is unavailable/held out; substituting revenue or EBITDA is an adaptation, not replication. Financial rank retrieval should be evaluated independently, not restricted to a cosine shortlist.\n\n### E17. Behavioral relationships can predict valuation similarity\n\n[Lee–Ma–Wang (2015), Table 4](https://www.hbs.edu/ris/Publication%20Files/13-048_0e7a49b1-bbbf-4715-bd91-3c6bc3150be2.pdf) reports EV/sales explanatory R-squared of .234 for GICS, .403 for ten equally weighted co-search peers and .439 for traffic-weighted peers. The study averages about 795 firms per quarter over 2009–2011. This is explanatory fit, not percentage valuation error or whole-company relevance. The [SEC's current log schema](https://www.sec.gov/files/variables-edgar-log-file-data-sets.pdf) lacks visitor identifiers in 2020-and-later files, preventing a straightforward current replication. A typed relationship graph would be a distinct adaptation.\n\n### E18. Human-reviewed multi-label business taxonomies\n\n[Tracxn's tagging workflow](https://w.tracxn.com/faqs/how-do-we-tag-companies-into-different-business-models) combines automated industry assignment, researcher sector classification and specialist business-model/submodel tagging. Its [annual report](https://cdn.tracxn.com/investor-relations/disclosures-to-stock-exchanges/Intimation__of_Annual_Report_ebUIBrOJrbry5LMXJraL9.pdf) describes multi-node company coverage. A [university deck, pages 4 and 17](https://www.uni-corvinus.hu/contents/uploads/2024/04/Tracxn-University-Proposal.pdf) distinguishes Uber from conventional taxis and Airbnb from Hilton. This is evidence of a production taxonomy and review workflow, not a controlled accuracy result. Multiple labels preserve broader scope but still require reliable extraction and appropriate granularity.\n\n### E19. Semantic search and supervised company lists serve different roles\n\n[DataCity's July 2024 R&D account](https://thedatacity.com/blog/building-the-industry-engine-similarity-score-update/) describes lexical ambiguity such as record shops versus gyms mentioning records, and missed similar recruiters with different wording. Semantic search complements its ML Lists trained with dozens of positive/negative company examples; it does not replace them. A [worked search example](https://thedatacity.com/blog/how-to-find-similar-companies-with-the-data-city/) links XR Games to Fallen Planet Studios and Preloaded. These are vendor examples without a controlled accuracy benchmark. They motivate separate tests of semantic retrieval and task-trained positive/hard-negative models.\n\n### E20. Source co-occurrence, review and operating constraints in acquisition workflows\n\n[SourceScrub's human-in-the-loop guide, pages 6–9](https://live.sourcescrub.com/hubfs/Guide/Sourcescrub_HumanintheLoop_Guide_2024.pdf) describes source co-occurrence, models and expert checks. Its [Synacti case](https://live.sourcescrub.com/hubfs/Case%20Study/Sourcescrub_CaseStudy-Synacti-RollUp_2025.pdf?cid=701VW00000Rf12NYAR) combines ownership, employee and software filters and credits the platform with discovering Callbell, acquired in December 2024. Claimed doubled efficiency and GBP 120,000 savings are customer/vendor testimony, not peer precision or a controlled model comparison. The workflow supports investigating distinct relationship evidence and eligibility constraints.\n\n### E21. Website-trained representations give competing answers across objectives\n\nThe [authors' library](https://hobergphillips.tuck.dartmouth.edu/tnic_doc2vec.html) labels this a 2024 working paper; the [manuscript](https://facultynew.tuck.dartmouth.edu/uploads/gordonPhillips/publications/BOKN_Journal___INFORMS_MnSc.pdf) indicates Management Science submission, not verified publication. It uses depth-three websites and expanded training from 3,800 public/32,000 private firms, with 300-dimensional PV-DBOW and global top-2% pair selection. Table 2 profit-fit adjusted R-squared/competitor recall: BoW-Jaccard .2497/13.53%; Doc2Vec-cosine .4180/30.20%; Doc2Vec-Siamese .2823/23.26%; RoBERTa-cosine .3455/11.10%; NAICS6 .3540/36.27%. The Siamese target is TNIC scores. Table 3's topic penalty raises same-sector share 72%→86% while lowering profit/assets R-squared .492→.469. These are global-network measures with incomplete competitor labels, not held-out top-five valuation. Training on 560 firms is a new condition. Filing-based ETNIC is not website-based WT-NIC.\n\nThe enterprise cases establish that these workflows are used, with different degrees of human review; they do not establish which method would win on our labels. The academic cases distinguish competitor recovery, human overlap, relational ranking, return correlations and valuation errors. Their metrics cannot be merged into a universal similarity leaderboard.")
